PHASE 3 — OPERATIONS
════════════════════
tool:  curl   (yes, just curl)

You found the bug in phase 2: jenkins-old serves the
descriptorByName/checkScript endpoint without authentication, and
it actually evaluates expressions sent in the `value` parameter.
That's the door. Now we walk through it.

The blueprint we want is stored on jenkins-old at:

    /var/m0use/blueprint.txt

Your job: use the unauth Groovy endpoint to read that file and
print its contents back to your screen. Then submit the flag from
inside the file.


THE EXPLOIT, IN PLAIN ENGLISH
─────────────────────────────
The vulnerable endpoint accepts a parameter called `value`. Whatever
you put in `value`, the server evaluates as Groovy code and prints
the result. So if you ask it to read a file, it'll read the file.

A Groovy expression that reads a file's contents:

    new File("/var/m0use/blueprint.txt").text

You need to pass that expression as the `value=` parameter on the
checkScript URL. Spaces and quotes have to be URL-encoded.


YOUR FIRST CURL
───────────────
Try this — start small. Ask the server to print a number:

    curl 'http://10.4.12.1:8080/jenkins/securityRealm/user/admin/descriptorByName/org.jenkinsci.plugins.scriptsecurity.sandbox.groovy.SecureGroovyScript/checkScript?value=println(42)'

That URL is one continuous string, no spaces. If it works you'll
see "Result: 42" — proof the server evaluated your code without
asking who you were.

Now do the same thing but ask it to read the blueprint file.

Read the response carefully. The flag is inside the file's text.
Type it straight at the prompt and hit Enter — same as before, no
keyword needed.

    <the-flag-string>

Stuck?  cat hint  — no penalty.
